From 668d5681ac83a3a690f2385547ce53c84f986ef0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vernon Mauery <vernon.mauery@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:30:38 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] rakp: reject RAKP3 against stale or default key material

RAKP Message 3 validated the remote console's HMAC against whatever
authentication state happened to be in the session, without confirming
that RAKP Message 1/2 had committed fresh material for it. Every
early-return path in RAKP1 (for example a request for CALLBACK
privilege, which returns 0x0a) bails out before the per-account user
key and a freshly generated BMC random number are committed, leaving a
constant default key ("0penBmc") and a stale/uninitialized BMC random
behind. RAKP3 then validated against that state, letting an
unauthenticated attacker activate a session with no valid password.

 - Zero-initialize the user key and BMC/console random numbers
 - Track whether RAKP2 committed fresh key material
 - Reject RAKP3 unless the session is still in setupInProgress, so an
   active session cannot re-run activation.
 - Add regression tests covering the RAKP3 state guard.

Tested: added unit test cases for the RAKP3 setup guard
        After applying fix, passwordless RAKP3 activation fails

Upstream-Status: Backport [Security, https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-net-ipmid/+/97459]
Fixes: CVE-2026-16141
Change-Id: I0e4b49c10d5b0d4e85d93b68aced8c076af7f9e5
Signed-off-by: Vernon Mauery <vernon.mauery@gmail.com>
Reported-by: HD Moore
